From 9544529366d5dee2c60fab3cdb735aff66314d42 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 08:05:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(funnel): put the qualifier on the homepage, publish the
 binding-constraint dataset
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0011. The engine, the deliverables and the subscription all existed; the
funnel that feeds them did not. /qualify was reachable only by typing the URL, and
nothing on the site sold the engagements the engine can actually produce.

THE QUALIFIER IS NOW ON THE HOMEPAGE

A #qualify section between the simulator and the services block — the narrative
place for it, since everything above is argument and this is the engine answering a
question about the reader's own hall for nothing. It is also the only thing on the
site that feeds the dataset.

THE DATASET IS NOW PUBLISHED (/api/insights)

The distribution of what actually binds across every hall run through the engine,
with the median tap-off rating, busway ampacity and plant supply temperature behind
it. Anonymised, cached, and rendered on the homepage and /qualify.

This is the growth loop and the moat in the same object. Anyone competent can
rebuild the constraint physics from ASHRAE, OCP and a month of work; nobody else is
accumulating a record of what stops real European halls, and every free run adds a
row. Publishing it is what turns a private asset into the reason this practice is
the one people call.

Two rules, both enforced in the route:
  * nothing identifying leaves it — no company, site, hall or email;
  * nothing is published below eight halls, because a distribution over four is
    noise dressed as evidence AND a small sample can point at whoever filled it in.
Below the threshold the component renders the honest version — how many halls are
in and what it takes to publish — rather than a chart of four rows.

The basis line states that the sample is self-selected: people run this because
they suspect a problem, so it is what binds among halls someone was worried about,
not a survey of the European estate. A dataset presented as more than it is would
be worth less than none.

THE ENGAGEMENT LADDER IS ON /pricing

Screen, Study deposit, Portfolio deposit and Hall Watch, each with its fee, what
the client receives and a working checkout button, read from lib/products.ts so the
page can never drift from what Stripe charges. Fixed fees throughout: a fee that
moved with our effort would make our slowness the client's problem.

Chart marks are now design tokens (chart / chart-alt in tailwind.config.ts) rather
than one-off hexes, so the dimmed, validated steps are reused rather than
re-derived. Sitemap now carries /qualify, /pricing, /infrastructure, /intelligence;
the section rail carries the qualifier.

155 Python tests pass. tsc, next lint and next build clean. Both new sections
rendered headlessly and reviewed before shipping.

